{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/NITVAustralia/posts/opinion-the-myth-of-a-flora-and-fauna-act-has-gained-traction-due-to-two-factors/10155457347442005/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/NITVAustralia/posts/opinion-the-myth-of-a-flora-and-fauna-act-has-gained-traction-due-to-two-factors/10155457347442005/","title":"NITV","description":"OPINION: The myth of a Flora and Fauna Act has gained traction due to two factors: high-profile people making the claim and that it's just so easy to believe, writes academic Samuel Brynand.","language":"en","text":"OPINION: The myth of a Flora and Fauna Act has gained traction due to two factors: high-profile people making the claim and that it's just so easy to believe, writes academic Samuel Brynand.\n\nAll reactions:\n\n49 comments\n\n57 shares\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nNITV limited who can comment on this post.\n\nMost relevant\n\n\n\nBen Burns\n\n'Aboriginal kids don’t need to hear fake news about their people’s history. There is enough legitimate horror and continuing suffering that needs to be addressed without being distracted by falsehoods.'\n\nCorrect.\n\n11\n\nMelinda Hogeboom\n\nFlora and Fauna Act myth or not she still made very valid points about Aboriginal people needing white Australia to stop dictating to them. We need to recognize Aboriginal people as equals and stop putting them down and treating them as second-class citizens.\n\n3\n\nPhillip Errington\n\nMore whitewash to oppress Aboriginal first nation people, bogus argument was there a Flora and Flora act specific to the Indigenous population of Australia. Australia wasn't Federated until 1901 and with that the expressed exclusion of Aboriginal people to citizenship of the newly formed Nation before and after Federation Aboriginal people were\n\n2\n\nMoorditj Yorga\n\nHere are some truths regarding Indigenous Australia.\n\nFact: Aboriginals were not considered equal citizens of Australia until 1967 and we were still regulated under Flora and Fauna. Several states would often manage Aboriginal affairs through departments that also handled flora, fauna and wildlife. We, as Aboriginal Australians were still being counted amongst the cattle right up until the 1967 Referendum. We were not seen as, or treated as, human beings.\n\nFact: Discriminatory State Laws and practices which often regulated many policies and procedures of Aboriginal Australia through Flora and Fauna, meant that Aboriginal people were unable to leave the reservation without a certificate of permission from the Native Welfare Officer. These certificates were commonly called 'dog licences'. Yes. We were tagged as sub-human and treated more like animals.\n\nFact: The Federal Constitution, written in 1900, explicitly stated that Aboriginals would not be counted in any state or federal census.\n\nFact: Queensland was the last state in Australia to grant state voting rights to Aboriginals in 1965; Aboriginals in the Northern Territory were considered “wards of the state” and were not allowed to vote in federal elections unless they were ex-servicemen up until 1962.\n\nFact: Voting and citizenship rights for Aboriginals were written into the constitution with a 1967 referendum, which also removed discriminatory references to Aboriginals from the Constitution and gave Parliament the power to make laws pertaining to Indigenous Peoples (previous to that, State Governments had total law-making power over Aborigines).\n\nFact: The Referendum set a voting record, with 90.77 percent of the entire population voting in favor of it.\n\nTruth: Interestingly, the highest percentage of “no” votes were recorded in territories with the highest Aboriginal populations, suggesting that anti-Indigenous racism was still rampant in many areas of Australia (since the passage of the Race Discrimination Act in 1975, 10,5000 complaints have been filed with the Government, with more than 3,500 of those coming from Aboriginals and Torres Strait Islanders). However, though voting is mandatory for all Australian citizens, voting was not made compulsory for Aborigines until 1983.\n\n(Let that sink in for a moment.)\n\nFact: In 1966, the United Nations proclaimed the International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination.\n\nFact: In 1967, a Commonwealth Referendum was held in order to amend the Federal Constitution to allow Aboriginal People to be counted in the census as Australians. The referendum also gave the Commonwealth the power to legislate for Aboriginal affairs, which until that time had been the sole responsibility of the States.\n\nFact: The granting of citizenship rights in 1969 finally restored to Noongars the legal rights they had possessed prior to the 1905 Act.\n\n(Again, let me reiterate that we were not considered human beings or seen as citizens in our own country up until this point.)\n\nFact: Queensland has lagged behind in many Indigenous rights laws – Queensland Aboriginals could be forced to live on reserves until 1971, and could not own their own property until 1975.\n\nFact: In 1959, Aboriginals became eligible to receive pensions and maternity leave, but only if they were not “nomadic or primitive,” and often group payments were made to reserves or missions rather than individuals or communities.\n\nFact: Though many of these discriminatory laws were changed in the 1960s and 1970s, others were being created – legislation created in the 1970s requires that the estate of an Aboriginal who dies without leaving a will should be automatically put in the hands of a public trustee, rather than granted to the next of kin as is the case with non-Aboriginals. The Law Reform Committee recommended that this law be changed in 2008, and the Department of Indigenous Affairs confirmed in 2012 that they are “considering it.”\n\nFact: Also contentious is New South Wales’ Flora and Fauna Law under the National Parks and Wildlife Act. This law claims that the majority of Aboriginal artifacts are “property of the crown,” and claims jurisdiction over all Aboriginal heritage and culture. Aboriginals, understandably, object to our culture being regulated under an act meant to protect vegetation and wild animals. New South Wales is the only state in Australia not to have a stand-alone Aboriginal Heritage Act, and activists have been lobbying for one for the past thirty-plus years.\n\n22\n\nView 1 reply\n\nSarah Redshaw\n\nBut what about terra nullius? That contributes to the reality that many were treated like animals\n\n18\n\nLee Harro\n\nI remember being taught at school about the Flora and Fauna Act and that aboriginals fell under it. Even as a young kid I was a bit shocked ....\n\n5\n\nView 1 reply\n\nMadonna Thomson\n\nSadly we’ve been hearing fake news about altered historical information about our history from non aboriginal people for decades.\n\nWhether it is a continued misunderstanding of whether we came under a Flora and Fauna Act or not misses the reality that until my mothers generation, we had very limited access to white legislations and information to be educatedly informed and as part of our ever learning and adjusting nsrrative in the Australian context we might get some of that info wrong.\n\nBut it does not diminish the reality that we were not British citizens and not afforded the same human rights. We do not need the so called flora and fauna “myth”, as it is now termed to highlight the fact that we were non entities within the historical narrative of what we know as the Australia.\n\nNicole Kirby Shoveller\n\nI think the point is Aboriginal people were counted as flora and fauna, the argument about whether there was a flora and fauna act or not is a distraction\n\n1\n\nView all 2 replies\n\nDaphne Gogos\n\nI was not quite listening in school I just remember the fauna part","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1990.6861720010056,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}